# =============================================================================
#  Line endings
#
#  Without these rules git prints a wall of
#      warning: in the working copy of 'X', LF will be replaced by CRLF
#  on every "git add" on Windows, and CRLF/LF ends up inconsistent between
#  clones. The rules below make it explicit instead of guessed.
#
#  The important one: batch and PowerShell files MUST stay CRLF. A .bat with
#  LF endings can misbehave (goto/label parsing in particular), so they are
#  pinned to CRLF rather than normalised to LF.
# =============================================================================

# Default: text files are stored as LF in the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows scripts: keep the bytes exactly as they are. -text disables
# normalisation, so a CRLF .bat is stored CRLF and comes back CRLF - including
# from GitHub's "Download ZIP", which serves the stored bytes rather than a
# checked-out copy. ("text eol=crlf" would store LF and only convert on
# checkout, leaving the ZIP download with LF endings that batch may choke on.)
*.bat  -text
*.cmd  -text
*.ps1  -text

# Build outputs and binaries - never touch, never diff as text.
*.jar  binary
*.zip  binary
*.png  binary
*.gz   binary
